WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:06.370
Before the 2010 reboot film, there was the
original A-Team television series. From 1983

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.629
to 1987, the NBC show graced American screens
with guns-a-blazing action and a cast of characters

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:16.160
fans instantly fell in love with. Roll it
all together and toss in some memorable catchphrases,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.580
and it's no wonder The A-Team saw stellar
ratings and gained a cult following. But whatever

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.000
happened to the cast that made the show shine
so brightly? Let's get reacquainted with these

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.560
'80s acting icons and see what they're up
to now.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.260
George Peppard

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.280
With a lovable fierceness and a fat cigar
permanently wedged between his teeth, George

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:40.700
Peppard played team leader Colonel John "Hannibal"
Smith. When The A-Team wrapped in 1987, Peppard

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.629
continued acting, popping up in the TV movies
Man Against the Mob and Man Against the Mob:

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:46.700
The Chinatown Murders.

00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:51.700
Much like Hannibal, Peppard was a smoker,
a habit that led to his lung cancer diagnosis

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:57.940
in 1992. Sadly, he passed away just two years
later, at the age of 65, after contracting

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.980
pneumonia . Just before his death, however,
he finished filming the pilot episode for

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.640
a would-have-been Matlock spin-off, The P.I.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.060
Dwight Schultz

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.580
As Captain H.M. "Howling Mad" Murdock, Dwight
Schultz catapulted into mainstream fame. Since

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.060
then, he's appeared in a few television series,
most notably Star Trek: The Next Generation

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:22.740
and Star Trek: Voyager as Lieutenant Reginald
Barclay. He's also dabbled in film, including

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:28.770
1989's Fat Man and Little Boy and 1996's Star
Trek: First Contact. He even showed up for

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.250
a post-credits scene in the A-Team film. From
the late '90s onward, Schultz found his groove

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.080
in voice-over work.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.180
Mr. T

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.900
We pity the fool who tries to challenge Bosco
Albert "B. A." Baracus, as portrayed unforgettably

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.260
by Mr. T. Outside of The A-Team, Mr. T has
gotten into voice acting, appeared on other

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.760
mega-popular shows like Diff'rent Strokes,
and even taken a stab at professional wrestling.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.540
Unfortunately, in 1995, he was diagnosed with
T-cell lymphoma, but that hasn't stopped him

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.840
from acting. He has specialized in playing
himself, thanks to his big personality and

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.800
marketable presence. He can be seen in commercials
for World of Warcraft, Comcast, and Snickers.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.600
"Snickers! Get some nuts!"

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:16.720
While he no longer rocks his signature stacked
gold chains, he's kept the B.A. Baracus mohawk

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.380
and thick beard.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.340
Dirk Benedict

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.960
Seasoned actor Dirk Benedict played the the
slick, smooth-talking Lieutenant Templeton

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:30.430
"Faceman" Peck. After The A-Team's cancellation,
Benedict appeared in movies like Shadow Force

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.140
and Alaska. He even made his directorial and
screenwriting debut in 2001 with the drama

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.220
film Cahoots. In 2007, he appeared on the
British version of Celebrity Big Brother.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.400
In the decade since, not much has been seen
of Benedict, though he did make a cameo in

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.480
The A-Team film in 2010. Unfortunately, Benedict
wasn't exactly happy with the movie as his

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.900
role only lasted a few seconds.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:53.680
Lance LeGault

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:59.600
While Lance LeGault found substantial stardom
prior to The A-Team, his time as Roderick

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.750
Decker earned him a gold star with audiences
around the world. After his work as the gun-wielding

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.150
colonel was done, he played another colonel,
this time on Magnum, P.I. He went to guest

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:14.240
star on a variety of other shows, while his
film credits include 1997's Mortal Kombat:

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.970
Annihilation and a voice role in Disney's
Home on the Range. Sadly, LeGault passed away

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:23.741
in 2012, although he posthumously appeared
alongside Paul Rudd and Emile Hirsch in the

00:03:23.741 --> 00:03:26.500
2013 comedy-drama film Prince Avalanche.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.760
Robert Vaughn

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:35.100
General Hunt Stockwell had a knack for antagonism,
a no-nonsense demeanor, and an ability to

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.849
wear a pair of aviator-framed BluBlockers
like nobody's business. Veteran actor Robert

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:43.880
Vaughn slipped into the role with ease, bringing
the formidable bad guy to life in the show's

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.300
final season. With an already long list of
acting roles in his resume, Vaughn kept his

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.629
momentum at a crazy-fast speed once the show
stopped airing. He took to the stage as Juror

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:58.430
9 in Twelve Angry Men, appeared in a few episodes
of Law &amp; Order, and starred in films like

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.459
Motel Blue and Pootie Tang. Vaughn's steadiest
gig following The A-Team, however, was in

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:08.750
the British drama series Hustle, which ran
from 2004 to 2012. After a year-long battle

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:14.099
with leukemia, Vaughn passed away in November
2016, but we have no doubt his acting legacy

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:15.460
will live on for a long time.

